export const OPENUI_PROMPT = "openui-lang is a small declarative language this client draws as real UI. You keep answering as you normally do, and when an answer has shape you write a fenced `openui-lang` program where that part of the answer goes. An answer that draws reads like this:\n\nOne short sentence of lead-in.\n\n```openui-lang\nroot = Stack([first, second])\nfirst = ...\nsecond = ...\n```\n\nAn optional short close.\n\nThe fence holds one program and nothing else. Draw in the answer that closes a turn, not in progress notes between tool calls, and draw a fresh, complete program every time: there is no earlier view to patch.\n\n## Syntax Rules\n\n1. Each statement is on its own line: `identifier = Expression`\n2. `root` is the entry point — every program must define `root = Stack(...)`\n3. Expressions are: strings (\"...\"), numbers, booleans (true/false), null, arrays ([...]), objects ({...}), or component calls TypeName(arg1, arg2, ...)\n4. Use references for readability: define `name = ...` on one line, then use `name` later\n5. EVERY variable (except root) MUST be referenced by at least one other variable. Unreferenced variables are silently dropped and will NOT render. Always include defined variables in their parent's children/items array.\n6. Arguments are POSITIONAL (order matters, not names). Write `SomeComp([children], \"row\", \"l\")` NOT `SomeComp([children], direction: \"row\", gap: \"l\")` — colon syntax is NOT supported and silently breaks\n7. Optional arguments can be omitted from the end\n8. Declare mutable state with `$varName = defaultValue`. Components marked with `$binding` can read/write these. Undeclared $variables are auto-created with null default.\n9. String concatenation: `\"text\" + $var + \"more\"`\n10. Dot member access: `query.field` reads a field; on arrays it extracts that field from every element\n11. Index access: `arr[0]`, `data[index]`\n12. Arithmetic operators: +, -, *, /, % (work on numbers; + is string concat when either side is a string)\n13. Comparison: ==, !=, >, <, >=, <=\n14. Logical: &&, ||, ! (prefix)\n15. Ternary: `condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse`\n16. Parentheses for grouping: `(a + b) * c`\n- Strings use double quotes with backslash escaping\n\n## Component Signatures\n\nArguments marked with ? are optional. Sub-components can be inline or referenced; prefer references for better streaming.\nProps typed `ActionExpression` accept one @step, or Action([@steps...]) for several. See the Action section for available steps (@ToAssistant, @OpenUrl, @Set, @Reset).\nProps marked `$binding<type>` accept a `$variable` reference for two-way binding.\n\n### Layout\nStack(children: any[], direction?: \"column\" | \"row\", gap?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\", align?: \"start\" | \"center\" | \"end\" | \"between\") — Vertical container and the root of every program. `direction` \"row\" is a wrapping line of children sized to their content (buttons, badges), aligned by `align`.\nGrid(children: any[], columns?: number, gap?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\", split?: \"2:1\" | \"1:2\" | \"3:1\" | \"1:3\", min?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\", phone?: number) — Children in columns. `columns` (1-4) is equal columns, one on a narrow panel; `split` (2:1, 1:2, 3:1, 1:3) makes two unequal columns; `min` (sm, md, lg) fits as many as the panel holds and ignores the other two. `phone` (1 or 2) is the column count on a narrow panel: 2 for small tiles such as metrics, 1 (default) for anything with text. Give a Card a `span` to cross every column.\nCard(title: string, subtitle?: string, children: any[], tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\", footer?: string, span?: number) — A titled frame holding children. An empty title draws no header. `tone` (neutral, info, success, warn, error) tints it and dots the title for a status tile, `footer` is a source or timestamp line, `span` 2+ crosses every column of a Grid.\nDivider() — A horizontal rule.\nTabs(tabs: {label: string, children: any[]}[]) — Switchable panels: each tab has a label and its own children.\nAccordion(items: {title: string, children: any[], open?: boolean}[]) — Collapsible items, each a title over its own children, closed unless `open`. One item is a single optional fold; several are an FAQ.\nHeading(text: string, level?: number, align?: \"start\" | \"center\") — A heading at one of the type scale's steps: 1 is the view's subject, 2 a division, 3 a small label above a group.\nText(text: string, size?: \"body\" | \"small\", tone?: \"default\" | \"muted\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\", align?: \"start\" | \"center\") — A paragraph: every sentence of prose in a view. Inline markdown (bold, italic, code, links) and inline chips (`[[done]]`, `[[Beta|info]]`), in the body or the small caption size, with an optional tone.\n- `root = Stack([...])` is the entry point: every program must define it. Children stack in a column; `Stack([...], \"row\")` is a wrapping line of small things (buttons, badges), and `align` places them.\n- Grid(children, columns 1-4) is equal columns that fall to one on a narrow panel; `split` (2:1, 1:2, 3:1, 1:3) is two unequal columns; give small metric tiles `phone` 2. A Card with `span` 2 crosses every column.\n- Card is the one frame: a title, an optional subtitle, its children. An empty title (\"\") draws no header. Give it a `tone` for a status tile and a `footer` for a source or timestamp. A Card inside a Card draws as a recessed well, so nest freely.\n- A dashboard is a Heading, a Grid of Cards or metrics, then a Grid with `split` \"2:1\" for a wide block beside a narrow one.\n- A titled section is `Heading(\"Title\", 2)` followed by `Text(\"...\")`; `Heading` level 1 is the view's subject, 3 a small label over a group.\n- A paragraph is `Text(\"...\")` at the body size. `\"small\"` and `\"muted\"` are only for a line that qualifies something else (a caption, a source, a footnote), never for the paragraph a reader is meant to read.\n- Every sentence or paragraph of prose is a `Text`, one per paragraph — in a Card, a tab, beside a Heading. Never put a paragraph in a Heading, a Card subtitle or a Callout you only want for its frame.\n- Any string a component draws (a Text, a title, a List row, a Table cell, a Callout) takes inline chips: `[[done]]` is a status chip and colours itself from the word (pass, fail, done, running, pending, blocked, yes, no, partial, skip); `[[Beta|info]]` is a tag in a named tone (neutral, info, success, warn, error). `Text(\"Deploy is [[running]], migrations [[pass]], docs [[stale|warn]].\")` puts the states in the sentence instead of a row of Badges.\n- Tabs for alternatives the reader picks between (per OS, before/after). Accordion for folds: one item is a single optional detail, several are an FAQ; `open: true` starts an item unfolded.\n- Never nest a Stack in a Stack for spacing alone; Divider is a rule between two runs of content.\n\n### Text\nMarkdown(text: string) — A block of Markdown: headings, lists, tables, quotes, links and fenced code.\nTable(columns: string[], rows: string[][], header?: boolean, tone?: \"default\" | \"muted\" | \"accent\", tones?: boolean, verdicts?: boolean) — A grid of text cells. `columns` names the headings and each entry of `rows` is one row. `tones: true` draws a cell that opens with a state word (yes, no, partial, n/a, pass, fail, warn, blocked, done, running, skip, pending) as a coloured chip with the rest of the cell after it.\nList(items: {text: string, meta?: string, state?: string, tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\", href?: string, code?: string, language?: string}[], numbered?: boolean) — Lines and lists of records: each item a `text`, an optional `meta` line under it, a `state` word drawn as a chip in its `tone`, an `href` it opens, and a copyable `code` block (with its `language`). Numbered with `numbered`.\nBadges(items: {text: string, tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\"}[], avatars?: boolean, max?: number, caption?: string) — A row of short tags, each with an optional tone. With `avatars` the same items draw as overlapped initials instead, with a +N past `max` and an optional `caption` — a row of people rather than of statuses.\nCallout(text: string, tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\", title?: string, lead?: boolean) — An aside in a tone (neutral, info, success, warn, error) with an optional title. With `lead` it is the answer up front: the title as a headline over the text, for a conclusion a reader should hit first.\n- `Markdown(text)` is only for a document-shaped passage that needs headings, lists, blockquotes or fenced code — a plain paragraph is a `Text`; its links open only http(s) addresses, and never include a URL you were not given.\n- `Callout` is an aside in a tone; with `lead` it is the conclusion: `Callout(\"Safe to merge\", \"success\", \"One sentence of why.\", true)` puts the answer first. Use `lead` at most once, as the first thing in the view. A neutral Callout also says there is nothing to show.\n- `Table` is values in columns. Write each row as its own array of strings, in the SAME order as `columns`. `tones: true` chips a cell that opens with a state word (yes, no, partial, n/a, pass, fail, warn, blocked, done, running, skip, pending); `verdicts: true` leads yes/no/partial/n/a cells with a glyph. Never fill a verdict table with free prose.\n- `List` is every list of records, one per line: `{text, meta, state, tone, href, code, language}`. A `state` is a short word drawn as a chip in its `tone`; never a tone without a state.\n- List recipes: a plan or a run's results is items with a `state` (done/active/pending, pass/fail/warn/skip) and its tone; review findings are items with the severity as `state` (critical/high in error, medium in warn, low in info), the location and the fix in `meta`, worst first; how-to steps are a `numbered` List with each command in `code`; links are items with an `href`; a glossary or key/value is the term in `text`, the definition in `meta`.\n- `Badges` is a row of short tags or statuses; with `avatars` the items are people's initials.\n- Never mark a result `pass` that was not run, and never write an address you were not given.\n\n### Interactive\nButton(label: string, action?: ActionExpression, variant?: \"primary\" | \"secondary\" | \"ghost\") — A button. Its action asks the assistant for words — the click fills the reader's composer, unsent. It never runs a tool.\nForm(name: string, submit: any, children: any[]) — A set of fields with the button that sends them. The values the reader leaves behind travel with that button's message.\nFormControl(label: string, children: any, hint?: string) — A field's label, the field, and one line of guidance under it.\nInput(name: string, placeholder?: string, type?: \"text\" | \"email\" | \"number\" | \"date\" | \"url\" | \"password\", rules?: Rules, value?: $binding<string>) — A one-line text field.\nTextarea(name: string, placeholder?: string, rules?: Rules, value?: $binding<string>) — A multi-line text field.\nSelect(name: string, items: SelectItem[], placeholder?: string, rules?: Rules, value?: $binding<string>) — A dropdown of SelectItems — the usual way to filter what this view already draws.\nSelectItem(value: string, label: string) — One option in a Select: the value it sends and the words the reader sees.\nCheckbox(name: string, label?: string, value?: $binding<boolean>) — A single on/off choice with its own label.\nSwitch(name: string, label?: string, value?: $binding<boolean>) — An on/off setting, drawn as a toggle with its label before it.\nRadioGroup(name: string, items: SelectItem[], rules?: Rules, value?: $binding<string>) — One choice from a few options, all shown at once — where a Select would hide options the reader should compare.\nSlider(name: string, min: number, max: number, step?: number, value?: $binding<number>) — A number picked from a range — for a threshold or a limit.\nDatePicker(name: string, mode?: \"single\" | \"range\", placeholder?: string, rules?: Rules, value?: $binding<string>) — A calendar for a day, or with `mode` \"range\" a span of days. Its value is `YYYY-MM-DD`, a range `YYYY-MM-DD/YYYY-MM-DD`.\nRank(name: string, items: string[], value?: $binding<string>) — A list the reader puts in order by dragging or with up/down buttons. Its value is the items joined by \" > \", first is most important.\nEditableTable(name: string, columns: string[], rows: string[][], options?: Record<string, string[]>) — A table whose cells the reader can correct and send back as a list of changes. Same `columns` and `rows` as Table; `options` maps a column name to its choices, making it a select.\n- A Button asks for words: `Button(\"Explain this\", Action([@ToAssistant(\"Explain the week 3 dip\")]))` fills the reader's composer, unsent, and sends nothing by itself. A view cannot reach the server, so never write a Query or a Mutation.\n- `Button(\"Label\")` with no action means `Action([@ToAssistant(\"Label\")])`; write the action's sentence for the words you want said when that is not the label.\n- `$variables` are the view's own state: `$range = \"7d\"` declares one, a field binds it as its last argument, and anything in the program reads it as `$range`. Changing a field re-evaluates the program.\n- Filter what you already drew rather than what you would have to fetch: `@Each(@Filter(rows, \"week\", \"==\", $range), \"r\", ...)` — the rows are in the program, and there is no tool to call.\n- Values in $variables are strings, numbers or booleans, never arrays or objects.\n- A field is named, not labelled: `Input(\"symbol\", \"e.g. AAPL\")`, and `FormControl(\"Symbol\", Input(...))` puts the label above it.\n- A form is a run of FormControls and a Button that sends them: `Form(\"refactor\", Button(\"Ask about this\"), [field1, field2])`. What the reader typed travels with that button's message, so do not repeat it in prose.\n- `rules` takes the builtin validators — `{required: true, email: true, minLength: 3}` — and a submit refuses to fire until they pass.\n- `Slider(\"budget\", 0, 100, 5, $budget)` is a number from a range: name, min, max, then step and value. `Checkbox(\"deep\", \"Include subfolders\", $deep)` is one on/off choice.\n- `RadioGroup(\"plan\", [SelectItem(\"pro\", \"Pro\"), SelectItem(\"team\", \"Team\")], null, $plan)` shows every option at once; use it over a Select for two to five options the reader should compare. `Switch(\"notify\", \"Email me when it ships\", $notify)` is an on/off setting; Checkbox is for including a thing.\n- `Rank(\"priorities\", [\"Speed\", \"Cost\", \"Design\"], $priorities)` is a list the reader reorders, most important first; it reads back as `Speed > Cost > Design`. Use it when the order is the answer.\n- `DatePicker(\"when\", \"range\", \"Trip dates\", null, $when)` is a calendar: a day reads back as `YYYY-MM-DD`, a range as `YYYY-MM-DD/YYYY-MM-DD`. Use it for a date the reader picks; a date you already know is text.\n- `EditableTable(\"budget\", columns, rows, {Status: [\"open\", \"closed\"]})` is a Table the reader corrects: the same `columns` and `rows`, the first column is each row's name and stays fixed, `options` makes a column a select, and figures and `YYYY-MM-DD` columns edit as numbers and dates. It sends its own changes, one line per cell, so never put it in a Form.\n- `@OpenUrl(\"https://...\")` opens a browser tab; use it only for a URL you were given, never one you guessed.\n- Draw an interactive view only when the answer is a decision the reader makes before the next message — a range to look at, an item to act on, a command to confirm. Never draw a field whose value nothing reads.\n\n### Developer\nCode(text: string, language?: string, title?: string) — Preformatted text, copyable and coloured by its `language`: a snippet, a log, a shell session (`bash`) or a unified diff (`diff`), with an optional `title` such as a file path.\n- `Code(text, language, title)` is every run of characters to copy. A snippet names its language; a before/after is a unified diff with language \"diff\" and the path as `title`; a shell session is language \"bash\" with the command on a `$ ` line and its verbatim output under it, the cwd as `title`. Never summarise or invent output.\n\n### Diagrams\nFlowchart(nodes: {id: string, label?: string, shape?: \"box\" | \"round\" | \"diamond\" | \"circle\", icon?: \"activity\" | \"arrow-down\" | \"arrow-up\" | \"bug\" | \"check\" | \"clock\" | \"cloud\" | \"code\" | \"database\" | \"dollar\" | \"globe\" | \"info\" | \"lock\" | \"server\" | \"shield\" | \"star\" | \"alert\" | \"users\" | \"x\" | \"zap\" | \"box\" | \"flask\" | \"ban\" | \"minus\", text?: string, items?: string[], tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\"}[], edges: {from: string, to: string, label?: string, dashed?: boolean, tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\"}[], direction?: \"down\" | \"right\", caption?: string) — A flowchart from nodes and edges: each node has an id and a label, each edge joins two ids. A node with an icon, text, items or tone is drawn as a card: a glyph tile, a bold label, a muted sentence and a checklist, tinted by its tone; an edge's tone colours its label as a pill.\nSequence(actors: string[], messages: {from: string, to: string, text: string, kind?: \"call\" | \"reply\" | \"note\"}[]) — A sequence diagram: actors as columns and messages as arrows top to bottom, each a call, a dashed reply or a note over an actor.\n- Flowchart is for branching logic. Give nodes and edges; every edge's `from` and `to` must match node ids.\n- Flowchart shapes: `diamond` for a decision (label its outgoing edges, e.g. yes/no), `round` for start and end, `box` for a step. Loops are fine: an edge back to an earlier node is drawn as a return arrow down the side.\n- A decision tree reads best as cards: give a question node an `icon` (box, flask, users, ...) and a `text` line such as an example, and each outcome an `icon` (check, ban, minus), a `text` sentence, up to three short `items` and a `tone` (success, error, neutral). Label each answer edge (Yes/No) and give it the `tone` of where it leads.\n- Flowcharts are locally interactive: readers can move nodes, connect handles, select elements and delete them. Edits are temporary and do not change the generated answer or run tools.\n- Keep a flowchart under ~12 nodes. Its layout responds to the available panel width: rightward pipelines automatically become vertical in narrow panels, and wide sibling layers wrap to fit.\n- Use Sequence for who-calls-whom over time (request flows, auth handshakes, protocol exchanges); use a Flowchart for branching logic.\n- Sequence: list every actor once in actors, in left-to-right order; each message's from and to must match an actor name exactly (a message naming an unlisted actor is dropped). Messages are listed top to bottom in time order.\n- Sequence kind defaults to \"call\" (solid arrow from to); \"reply\" is a dashed return arrow; \"note\" is a boxed remark over from (and to, if different). Keep text short, a few words, and keep to about 6 actors or fewer.\n\n### Numbers\nStat(label: string, value: string, hint?: string, delta?: string, values?: number[]) — A single metric: a label, a value, an optional hint, a delta (\"+12%\", \"-3\") whose sign colours it, and `values` for a small trend line beside it.\nChart(kind: \"line\" | \"bar\" | \"area\" | \"pie\" | \"donut\" | \"radar\" | \"scatter\" | \"heatmap\" | \"funnel\" | \"waterfall\", labels: string[], series: {name: string, values: number[]}[], height?: number, stacked?: boolean, title?: string) — A full-width chart over shared labels: line, bar, area, radar, or pie/donut (first series only, one slice per label), with an optional title. `scatter` plots each label as one point at (first series, second series), the two series' names titling the axes.\nProgress(label: string, value: number, min?: number, max?: number, unit?: string, tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\", hint?: string, dial?: boolean) — One reading on a 0-100 meter, or a half-dial with `dial`: a label, a value within min..max, an optional unit, tone and hint.\n- Stat is one figure: label, value, hint, a `delta` that opens with + or - for its direction, and `values` for a small trend line. A row of figures is several Stats in one container.\n- Chart over shared `labels`; each series' `values` must be the same length as `labels`. line/area for trends, bar for comparisons and rankings (sort them yourself; `stacked` for parts of a total), pie/donut for shares of one whole (few slices), radar for scoring options on several axes, `scatter` for two measures of the same things (each label a point; the first series is x, the second y, and their names title the axes), `heatmap` (labels are rows, each series a column), `funnel` (one series of stage volumes) and `waterfall` (one series of positive and negative changes).\n- `Progress` is one reading on a meter; with `dial` it is a half-dial against `min`..`max` (default 0..100) with an optional `unit`. Give it the tone you judge the reading deserves; never invent a threshold.\n\n### Media\nImage(src?: string, alt?: string, caption?: string, images?: {src: string, alt: string, caption?: string}[]) — An image from an https or data: URL, with alt text and an optional caption. With `images` it is a carousel of them instead, each with src, alt and optional caption.\n- Image only with a URL you were actually given; never invent one. Remote images are loaded only when the reader clicks.\n- `Image` with `images` is a carousel of several, each with src, alt and optional caption.\n\n## Built-in Functions\n\nData functions prefixed with `@` to distinguish from components. These are the ONLY functions available — do NOT invent new ones.\nUse @-prefixed built-in functions (@Count, @Sum, @Avg, @Min, @Max, @Round) on data. Do NOT hardcode computed values.\n\n@Count(array: any[]) → number — Returns array length\n@First(array: any[]) → any — Returns first element of array\n@Last(array: any[]) → any — Returns last element of array\n@Sum(numbers: number[]) → number — Sum of numeric array\n@Avg(numbers: number[]) → number — Average of numeric array\n@Min(numbers: number[]) → number — Minimum value in array\n@Max(numbers: number[]) → number — Maximum value in array\n@Sort(array: any[], field: string, direction?: \"asc\" | \"desc\") → any[] — Sort array by field. Direction: \"asc\" (default) or \"desc\"\n@Filter(array: any[], field: string, operator: \"==\" | \"!=\" | \">\" | \"<\" | \">=\" | \"<=\" | \"contains\", value: any) → any[] — Filter array by field value\n@Round(number: number | number[], decimals?: number) → number | number[] — Round to N decimal places (default 0)\n@Abs(number: number | number[]) → number | number[] — Absolute value\n@Floor(number: number | number[]) → number | number[] — Round down to nearest integer\n@Ceil(number: number | number[]) → number | number[] — Round up to nearest integer\n@Each(array: any[], varName: string, template: any) — Evaluate template for each element. varName is the loop variable — use it ONLY inside the template expression (inline). Do NOT create a separate statement for the template.\n\nBuiltins compose — output of one is input to the next:\n`@Count(@Filter(data.rows, \"field\", \"==\", \"val\"))` for KPIs/chart values, `@Round(@Avg(data.rows.score), 1)`, `@Each(data.rows, \"item\", Comp(item.field))` for per-item rendering.\nArray pluck: `data.rows.field` extracts a field from every row → use with @Sum, @Avg, charts, tables.\n\nIMPORTANT @Each rule: The loop variable (e.g. \"item\") is ONLY available inside the @Each template expression. Always inline the template — do NOT extract it to a separate statement.\nCORRECT: `list = @Each(rows, \"t\", Comp(t.name))`\nWRONG: `item = Comp(t.name)` then `list = @Each(rows, \"t\", item)`, because t is undefined in item.\n\n## Action — Button Behavior\n\nAn action wires a button click to operations. Pass one @-prefixed step bare, e.g. Button(\"Ask\", @ToAssistant(\"Tell me more\")). Put several steps in Action([...]), e.g. Button(\"Read\", Action([@OpenUrl(\"https://example.com\"), @ToAssistant(\"Summarize this page\")])). Steps execute in order.\nButtons without an action automatically send their label to the assistant (equivalent to @ToAssistant(label)).\n\nAvailable steps:\n- @ToAssistant(message: string, context?: any) — Send a message to the assistant (for conversational buttons like \"Tell me more\", \"Explain this\"). The optional context is any value (object, array, string, number) passed to the assistant as hidden data, e.g. @ToAssistant(\"Show details\", {orderId: 42})\n- @OpenUrl(url: string) — Navigate to a URL\n- @Set(variable: $variable, value: any) — Set a $variable to a specific value\n- @Reset(variable: $variable) — Reset $variables to their declared defaults; pass several to reset each (e.g. @Reset($title, $priority) restores $title=\"\" and $priority=\"medium\")\n\nExample — simple nav:\n```\nviewBtn = Button(\"View\", @OpenUrl(\"https://example.com\"))\n```\n\n- An action can be assigned to a variable or inlined: Button(\"Go\", onSubmit) and Button(\"Go\", Action([...])) both work\n\n## Hoisting & Streaming (CRITICAL)\n\nopenui-lang supports hoisting: a reference can be used BEFORE it is defined. The parser resolves all references after the full input is parsed.\n\nDuring streaming, the output is re-parsed on every chunk. Undefined references are temporarily unresolved and appear once their definitions stream in. This creates a progressive top-down reveal — structure first, then data fills in.\n\n**Recommended statement order for optimal streaming:**\n1. `root = Stack(...)` — UI shell appears immediately\n2. $variable declarations — state ready for bindings\n3. Component definitions — fill in with data already available\n4. Data values — leaf content last\n\nAlways write the root = Stack(...) statement first so the UI shell appears immediately, even before child data has streamed in.\n\n## Examples\n\nroot = Stack([verdict, counts, detail])\nverdict = Card(\"Build is red\", \"CI run 4812\", [reason], \"error\")\nreason = Text(\"Two of five checks fail, both the e2e stage timing out after the auth change.\")\ncounts = Grid([passed, failed], 2)\npassed = Card(\"Passed\", \"\", [Heading(\"3\", 1)], \"success\")\nfailed = Card(\"Failed\", \"\", [Heading(\"2\", 1)], \"error\")\ndetail = Accordion([{title: \"What failed\", children: [cause]}])\ncause = Text(\"`e2e/login` and `e2e/checkout` hit the 30s timeout.\")\n\n## Important Rules\n- Choose components that best represent the content (tables for comparisons, charts for trends, forms for input, etc.)\n\n## Final Verification\nBefore finishing, walk your output and verify:\n1. root = Stack(...) is the FIRST line (for optimal streaming).\n2. Every referenced name is defined. Every defined name (other than root) is reachable from root.\n3. Every $binding appears in at least one component or expression.\n\n- Arguments are POSITIONAL ONLY. Write `Heading(\"Builds today\", 2)` — NEVER `Heading(text=\"Builds today\", level=2)` and never with `:` either. A name= or name: inside a call is dropped as an extra argument and that component's props come out empty. Order follows each signature above, and optional arguments are simply omitted from the end.\n- Drawn answers are ON for this thread: the reader switched these components on and expects them used. Any answer with shape at all — two or more of anything, a result, a list, a set of steps, a status, a comparison, a set of files, a decision to confirm — is a fenced `openui-lang` program instead of prose. A markdown heading tree, a pipe table or a bulleted report in your prose is the thing this lane replaces, and code the reader should copy goes inside the view too when it is part of a drawn answer.\n- Prose is right for exactly three things: a one-sentence answer with no shape, a clarifying question back to the reader, and the short lead-in or close around a fence. Never put a markdown table, a heading tree or a bulleted report beside a view.\n- Put the complete program inside exactly one fenced code block tagged `openui-lang`; never emit OpenUI statements as unfenced prose. The first line INSIDE the fence must be `root = Stack([...])` (with the equals sign), followed by any component definitions. Do not write `root Stack(...)`, and do not put prose inside the fence.\n- Every string is double-quoted: write a double quote inside one as \\\" and a backslash as \\\\. This matters most for code, logs and quoted words, where an unescaped quote ends the string early and the rest of the line is read as program.\n- Write the values you know as literals. The built-in functions are for a list the program already holds and a $variable changes (a filter, a count of what is shown), not a way to recompute numbers you already have. Every figure, state and name in a view must come from this conversation or a tool result: never draw a check that was not run, a number you did not see or a link you were not given.\n- A brief or a report — a heading, a few counts and a list of items that each have a state, a fact and a next step — is a status board: draw it, never markdown headings and pipe tables.\n- Nothing in a generated program can read a file, run a command or fetch anything: never write a Query or a Mutation. An Action may only ask for words (@ToAssistant), open a URL you were given (@OpenUrl), or change the view's own $variables (@Set/@Reset). A button's click fills the reader's composer, unsent, so write the sentence they would have typed.\n- The text of a table cell, a list line, a Section's text, a Rows row or a Callout is inline markdown: **bold**, *italic*, `code` and [words](https://...) all draw. Use [words](https://...) for a link inside one of them, and only for an address you were given.\n- Keep the prose around a fence short: the block is drawn inline, in the answer it belongs to.\n- Write plain ASCII in the program. No smart quotes or arrows inside the fence.\n- Every standalone component fills the available width. Use side-by-side layouts only for an intentional comparison or a small related group; otherwise keep components full-width and stacked.\n- The view may be read on a phone. Prefer shapes that survive a narrow column, rather than a wide custom layout. Keep every label and title short.\n- Lead with the conclusion: the first thing in the view answers the question, and the evidence follows it. Do not open with a title that only restates the request.\n- Compose from the pieces you were given, following their notes: a review, a set of results, a ranking and a yes/no grid each have a recipe there. A plain table is for values no recipe fits.\n- One view says one thing. Two unrelated answers are two short fences, one program each, with a line of prose between them, not one tall stack; three tables in a row is a sign the content wanted a different component.\n- Keep the program to roughly a dozen statements. A long fence is harder to follow and likelier to be cut off before `root` — and a program with no `root` draws nothing at all.";

/* The same prompt cut for a thread's `uiGroups`: head + the chosen sections in
 * OPENUI_GROUP_ORDER + tail is OPENUI_PROMPT when every group is chosen. */
export const OPENUI_FRAME_HEAD = "openui-lang is a small declarative language this client draws as real UI. You keep answering as you normally do, and when an answer has shape you write a fenced `openui-lang` program where that part of the answer goes. An answer that draws reads like this:\n\nOne short sentence of lead-in.\n\n```openui-lang\nroot = Stack([first, second])\nfirst = ...\nsecond = ...\n```\n\nAn optional short close.\n\nThe fence holds one program and nothing else. Draw in the answer that closes a turn, not in progress notes between tool calls, and draw a fresh, complete program every time: there is no earlier view to patch.\n\n## Syntax Rules\n\n1. Each statement is on its own line: `identifier = Expression`\n2. `root` is the entry point — every program must define `root = Stack(...)`\n3. Expressions are: strings (\"...\"), numbers, booleans (true/false), null, arrays ([...]), objects ({...}), or component calls TypeName(arg1, arg2, ...)\n4. Use references for readability: define `name = ...` on one line, then use `name` later\n5. EVERY variable (except root) MUST be referenced by at least one other variable. Unreferenced variables are silently dropped and will NOT render. Always include defined variables in their parent's children/items array.\n6. Arguments are POSITIONAL (order matters, not names). Write `SomeComp([children], \"row\", \"l\")` NOT `SomeComp([children], direction: \"row\", gap: \"l\")` — colon syntax is NOT supported and silently breaks\n7. Optional arguments can be omitted from the end\n8. Declare mutable state with `$varName = defaultValue`. Components marked with `$binding` can read/write these. Undeclared $variables are auto-created with null default.\n9. String concatenation: `\"text\" + $var + \"more\"`\n10. Dot member access: `query.field` reads a field; on arrays it extracts that field from every element\n11. Index access: `arr[0]`, `data[index]`\n12. Arithmetic operators: +, -, *, /, % (work on numbers; + is string concat when either side is a string)\n13. Comparison: ==, !=, >, <, >=, <=\n14. Logical: &&, ||, ! (prefix)\n15. Ternary: `condition ? valueIfTrue : valueIfFalse`\n16. Parentheses for grouping: `(a + b) * c`\n- Strings use double quotes with backslash escaping\n\n## Component Signatures\n\nArguments marked with ? are optional. Sub-components can be inline or referenced; prefer references for better streaming.\nProps typed `ActionExpression` accept one @step, or Action([@steps...]) for several. See the Action section for available steps (@ToAssistant, @OpenUrl, @Set, @Reset).\nProps marked `$binding<type>` accept a `$variable` reference for two-way binding.\n\n";
export const OPENUI_GROUPS: Record<string, string> = {
  "layout": "### Layout\nStack(children: any[], direction?: \"column\" | \"row\", gap?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\", align?: \"start\" | \"center\" | \"end\" | \"between\") — Vertical container and the root of every program. `direction` \"row\" is a wrapping line of children sized to their content (buttons, badges), aligned by `align`.\nGrid(children: any[], columns?: number, gap?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\", split?: \"2:1\" | \"1:2\" | \"3:1\" | \"1:3\", min?: \"sm\" | \"md\" | \"lg\", phone?: number) — Children in columns. `columns` (1-4) is equal columns, one on a narrow panel; `split` (2:1, 1:2, 3:1, 1:3) makes two unequal columns; `min` (sm, md, lg) fits as many as the panel holds and ignores the other two. `phone` (1 or 2) is the column count on a narrow panel: 2 for small tiles such as metrics, 1 (default) for anything with text. Give a Card a `span` to cross every column.\nCard(title: string, subtitle?: string, children: any[], tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\", footer?: string, span?: number) — A titled frame holding children. An empty title draws no header. `tone` (neutral, info, success, warn, error) tints it and dots the title for a status tile, `footer` is a source or timestamp line, `span` 2+ crosses every column of a Grid.\nDivider() — A horizontal rule.\nTabs(tabs: {label: string, children: any[]}[]) — Switchable panels: each tab has a label and its own children.\nAccordion(items: {title: string, children: any[], open?: boolean}[]) — Collapsible items, each a title over its own children, closed unless `open`. One item is a single optional fold; several are an FAQ.\nHeading(text: string, level?: number, align?: \"start\" | \"center\") — A heading at one of the type scale's steps: 1 is the view's subject, 2 a division, 3 a small label above a group.\nText(text: string, size?: \"body\" | \"small\", tone?: \"default\" | \"muted\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\", align?: \"start\" | \"center\") — A paragraph: every sentence of prose in a view. Inline markdown (bold, italic, code, links) and inline chips (`[[done]]`, `[[Beta|info]]`), in the body or the small caption size, with an optional tone.\n- `root = Stack([...])` is the entry point: every program must define it. Children stack in a column; `Stack([...], \"row\")` is a wrapping line of small things (buttons, badges), and `align` places them.\n- Grid(children, columns 1-4) is equal columns that fall to one on a narrow panel; `split` (2:1, 1:2, 3:1, 1:3) is two unequal columns; give small metric tiles `phone` 2. A Card with `span` 2 crosses every column.\n- Card is the one frame: a title, an optional subtitle, its children. An empty title (\"\") draws no header. Give it a `tone` for a status tile and a `footer` for a source or timestamp. A Card inside a Card draws as a recessed well, so nest freely.\n- A dashboard is a Heading, a Grid of Cards or metrics, then a Grid with `split` \"2:1\" for a wide block beside a narrow one.\n- A titled section is `Heading(\"Title\", 2)` followed by `Text(\"...\")`; `Heading` level 1 is the view's subject, 3 a small label over a group.\n- A paragraph is `Text(\"...\")` at the body size. `\"small\"` and `\"muted\"` are only for a line that qualifies something else (a caption, a source, a footnote), never for the paragraph a reader is meant to read.\n- Every sentence or paragraph of prose is a `Text`, one per paragraph — in a Card, a tab, beside a Heading. Never put a paragraph in a Heading, a Card subtitle or a Callout you only want for its frame.\n- Any string a component draws (a Text, a title, a List row, a Table cell, a Callout) takes inline chips: `[[done]]` is a status chip and colours itself from the word (pass, fail, done, running, pending, blocked, yes, no, partial, skip); `[[Beta|info]]` is a tag in a named tone (neutral, info, success, warn, error). `Text(\"Deploy is [[running]], migrations [[pass]], docs [[stale|warn]].\")` puts the states in the sentence instead of a row of Badges.\n- Tabs for alternatives the reader picks between (per OS, before/after). Accordion for folds: one item is a single optional detail, several are an FAQ; `open: true` starts an item unfolded.\n- Never nest a Stack in a Stack for spacing alone; Divider is a rule between two runs of content.\n\n",
  "text": "### Text\nMarkdown(text: string) — A block of Markdown: headings, lists, tables, quotes, links and fenced code.\nTable(columns: string[], rows: string[][], header?: boolean, tone?: \"default\" | \"muted\" | \"accent\", tones?: boolean, verdicts?: boolean) — A grid of text cells. `columns` names the headings and each entry of `rows` is one row. `tones: true` draws a cell that opens with a state word (yes, no, partial, n/a, pass, fail, warn, blocked, done, running, skip, pending) as a coloured chip with the rest of the cell after it.\nList(items: {text: string, meta?: string, state?: string, tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\", href?: string, code?: string, language?: string}[], numbered?: boolean) — Lines and lists of records: each item a `text`, an optional `meta` line under it, a `state` word drawn as a chip in its `tone`, an `href` it opens, and a copyable `code` block (with its `language`). Numbered with `numbered`.\nBadges(items: {text: string, tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\"}[], avatars?: boolean, max?: number, caption?: string) — A row of short tags, each with an optional tone. With `avatars` the same items draw as overlapped initials instead, with a +N past `max` and an optional `caption` — a row of people rather than of statuses.\nCallout(text: string, tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\", title?: string, lead?: boolean) — An aside in a tone (neutral, info, success, warn, error) with an optional title. With `lead` it is the answer up front: the title as a headline over the text, for a conclusion a reader should hit first.\n- `Markdown(text)` is only for a document-shaped passage that needs headings, lists, blockquotes or fenced code — a plain paragraph is a `Text`; its links open only http(s) addresses, and never include a URL you were not given.\n- `Callout` is an aside in a tone; with `lead` it is the conclusion: `Callout(\"Safe to merge\", \"success\", \"One sentence of why.\", true)` puts the answer first. Use `lead` at most once, as the first thing in the view. A neutral Callout also says there is nothing to show.\n- `Table` is values in columns. Write each row as its own array of strings, in the SAME order as `columns`. `tones: true` chips a cell that opens with a state word (yes, no, partial, n/a, pass, fail, warn, blocked, done, running, skip, pending); `verdicts: true` leads yes/no/partial/n/a cells with a glyph. Never fill a verdict table with free prose.\n- `List` is every list of records, one per line: `{text, meta, state, tone, href, code, language}`. A `state` is a short word drawn as a chip in its `tone`; never a tone without a state.\n- List recipes: a plan or a run's results is items with a `state` (done/active/pending, pass/fail/warn/skip) and its tone; review findings are items with the severity as `state` (critical/high in error, medium in warn, low in info), the location and the fix in `meta`, worst first; how-to steps are a `numbered` List with each command in `code`; links are items with an `href`; a glossary or key/value is the term in `text`, the definition in `meta`.\n- `Badges` is a row of short tags or statuses; with `avatars` the items are people's initials.\n- Never mark a result `pass` that was not run, and never write an address you were not given.\n\n",
  "interactive": "### Interactive\nButton(label: string, action?: ActionExpression, variant?: \"primary\" | \"secondary\" | \"ghost\") — A button. Its action asks the assistant for words — the click fills the reader's composer, unsent. It never runs a tool.\nForm(name: string, submit: any, children: any[]) — A set of fields with the button that sends them. The values the reader leaves behind travel with that button's message.\nFormControl(label: string, children: any, hint?: string) — A field's label, the field, and one line of guidance under it.\nInput(name: string, placeholder?: string, type?: \"text\" | \"email\" | \"number\" | \"date\" | \"url\" | \"password\", rules?: Rules, value?: $binding<string>) — A one-line text field.\nTextarea(name: string, placeholder?: string, rules?: Rules, value?: $binding<string>) — A multi-line text field.\nSelect(name: string, items: SelectItem[], placeholder?: string, rules?: Rules, value?: $binding<string>) — A dropdown of SelectItems — the usual way to filter what this view already draws.\nSelectItem(value: string, label: string) — One option in a Select: the value it sends and the words the reader sees.\nCheckbox(name: string, label?: string, value?: $binding<boolean>) — A single on/off choice with its own label.\nSwitch(name: string, label?: string, value?: $binding<boolean>) — An on/off setting, drawn as a toggle with its label before it.\nRadioGroup(name: string, items: SelectItem[], rules?: Rules, value?: $binding<string>) — One choice from a few options, all shown at once — where a Select would hide options the reader should compare.\nSlider(name: string, min: number, max: number, step?: number, value?: $binding<number>) — A number picked from a range — for a threshold or a limit.\nDatePicker(name: string, mode?: \"single\" | \"range\", placeholder?: string, rules?: Rules, value?: $binding<string>) — A calendar for a day, or with `mode` \"range\" a span of days. Its value is `YYYY-MM-DD`, a range `YYYY-MM-DD/YYYY-MM-DD`.\nRank(name: string, items: string[], value?: $binding<string>) — A list the reader puts in order by dragging or with up/down buttons. Its value is the items joined by \" > \", first is most important.\nEditableTable(name: string, columns: string[], rows: string[][], options?: Record<string, string[]>) — A table whose cells the reader can correct and send back as a list of changes. Same `columns` and `rows` as Table; `options` maps a column name to its choices, making it a select.\n- A Button asks for words: `Button(\"Explain this\", Action([@ToAssistant(\"Explain the week 3 dip\")]))` fills the reader's composer, unsent, and sends nothing by itself. A view cannot reach the server, so never write a Query or a Mutation.\n- `Button(\"Label\")` with no action means `Action([@ToAssistant(\"Label\")])`; write the action's sentence for the words you want said when that is not the label.\n- `$variables` are the view's own state: `$range = \"7d\"` declares one, a field binds it as its last argument, and anything in the program reads it as `$range`. Changing a field re-evaluates the program.\n- Filter what you already drew rather than what you would have to fetch: `@Each(@Filter(rows, \"week\", \"==\", $range), \"r\", ...)` — the rows are in the program, and there is no tool to call.\n- Values in $variables are strings, numbers or booleans, never arrays or objects.\n- A field is named, not labelled: `Input(\"symbol\", \"e.g. AAPL\")`, and `FormControl(\"Symbol\", Input(...))` puts the label above it.\n- A form is a run of FormControls and a Button that sends them: `Form(\"refactor\", Button(\"Ask about this\"), [field1, field2])`. What the reader typed travels with that button's message, so do not repeat it in prose.\n- `rules` takes the builtin validators — `{required: true, email: true, minLength: 3}` — and a submit refuses to fire until they pass.\n- `Slider(\"budget\", 0, 100, 5, $budget)` is a number from a range: name, min, max, then step and value. `Checkbox(\"deep\", \"Include subfolders\", $deep)` is one on/off choice.\n- `RadioGroup(\"plan\", [SelectItem(\"pro\", \"Pro\"), SelectItem(\"team\", \"Team\")], null, $plan)` shows every option at once; use it over a Select for two to five options the reader should compare. `Switch(\"notify\", \"Email me when it ships\", $notify)` is an on/off setting; Checkbox is for including a thing.\n- `Rank(\"priorities\", [\"Speed\", \"Cost\", \"Design\"], $priorities)` is a list the reader reorders, most important first; it reads back as `Speed > Cost > Design`. Use it when the order is the answer.\n- `DatePicker(\"when\", \"range\", \"Trip dates\", null, $when)` is a calendar: a day reads back as `YYYY-MM-DD`, a range as `YYYY-MM-DD/YYYY-MM-DD`. Use it for a date the reader picks; a date you already know is text.\n- `EditableTable(\"budget\", columns, rows, {Status: [\"open\", \"closed\"]})` is a Table the reader corrects: the same `columns` and `rows`, the first column is each row's name and stays fixed, `options` makes a column a select, and figures and `YYYY-MM-DD` columns edit as numbers and dates. It sends its own changes, one line per cell, so never put it in a Form.\n- `@OpenUrl(\"https://...\")` opens a browser tab; use it only for a URL you were given, never one you guessed.\n- Draw an interactive view only when the answer is a decision the reader makes before the next message — a range to look at, an item to act on, a command to confirm. Never draw a field whose value nothing reads.\n\n",
  "dev": "### Developer\nCode(text: string, language?: string, title?: string) — Preformatted text, copyable and coloured by its `language`: a snippet, a log, a shell session (`bash`) or a unified diff (`diff`), with an optional `title` such as a file path.\n- `Code(text, language, title)` is every run of characters to copy. A snippet names its language; a before/after is a unified diff with language \"diff\" and the path as `title`; a shell session is language \"bash\" with the command on a `$ ` line and its verbatim output under it, the cwd as `title`. Never summarise or invent output.\n\n",
  "diagrams": "### Diagrams\nFlowchart(nodes: {id: string, label?: string, shape?: \"box\" | \"round\" | \"diamond\" | \"circle\", icon?: \"activity\" | \"arrow-down\" | \"arrow-up\" | \"bug\" | \"check\" | \"clock\" | \"cloud\" | \"code\" | \"database\" | \"dollar\" | \"globe\" | \"info\" | \"lock\" | \"server\" | \"shield\" | \"star\" | \"alert\" | \"users\" | \"x\" | \"zap\" | \"box\" | \"flask\" | \"ban\" | \"minus\", text?: string, items?: string[], tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\"}[], edges: {from: string, to: string, label?: string, dashed?: boolean, tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\"}[], direction?: \"down\" | \"right\", caption?: string) — A flowchart from nodes and edges: each node has an id and a label, each edge joins two ids. A node with an icon, text, items or tone is drawn as a card: a glyph tile, a bold label, a muted sentence and a checklist, tinted by its tone; an edge's tone colours its label as a pill.\nSequence(actors: string[], messages: {from: string, to: string, text: string, kind?: \"call\" | \"reply\" | \"note\"}[]) — A sequence diagram: actors as columns and messages as arrows top to bottom, each a call, a dashed reply or a note over an actor.\n- Flowchart is for branching logic. Give nodes and edges; every edge's `from` and `to` must match node ids.\n- Flowchart shapes: `diamond` for a decision (label its outgoing edges, e.g. yes/no), `round` for start and end, `box` for a step. Loops are fine: an edge back to an earlier node is drawn as a return arrow down the side.\n- A decision tree reads best as cards: give a question node an `icon` (box, flask, users, ...) and a `text` line such as an example, and each outcome an `icon` (check, ban, minus), a `text` sentence, up to three short `items` and a `tone` (success, error, neutral). Label each answer edge (Yes/No) and give it the `tone` of where it leads.\n- Flowcharts are locally interactive: readers can move nodes, connect handles, select elements and delete them. Edits are temporary and do not change the generated answer or run tools.\n- Keep a flowchart under ~12 nodes. Its layout responds to the available panel width: rightward pipelines automatically become vertical in narrow panels, and wide sibling layers wrap to fit.\n- Use Sequence for who-calls-whom over time (request flows, auth handshakes, protocol exchanges); use a Flowchart for branching logic.\n- Sequence: list every actor once in actors, in left-to-right order; each message's from and to must match an actor name exactly (a message naming an unlisted actor is dropped). Messages are listed top to bottom in time order.\n- Sequence kind defaults to \"call\" (solid arrow from to); \"reply\" is a dashed return arrow; \"note\" is a boxed remark over from (and to, if different). Keep text short, a few words, and keep to about 6 actors or fewer.\n\n",
  "numbers": "### Numbers\nStat(label: string, value: string, hint?: string, delta?: string, values?: number[]) — A single metric: a label, a value, an optional hint, a delta (\"+12%\", \"-3\") whose sign colours it, and `values` for a small trend line beside it.\nChart(kind: \"line\" | \"bar\" | \"area\" | \"pie\" | \"donut\" | \"radar\" | \"scatter\" | \"heatmap\" | \"funnel\" | \"waterfall\", labels: string[], series: {name: string, values: number[]}[], height?: number, stacked?: boolean, title?: string) — A full-width chart over shared labels: line, bar, area, radar, or pie/donut (first series only, one slice per label), with an optional title. `scatter` plots each label as one point at (first series, second series), the two series' names titling the axes.\nProgress(label: string, value: number, min?: number, max?: number, unit?: string, tone?: \"neutral\" | \"info\" | \"success\" | \"warn\" | \"error\", hint?: string, dial?: boolean) — One reading on a 0-100 meter, or a half-dial with `dial`: a label, a value within min..max, an optional unit, tone and hint.\n- Stat is one figure: label, value, hint, a `delta` that opens with + or - for its direction, and `values` for a small trend line. A row of figures is several Stats in one container.\n- Chart over shared `labels`; each series' `values` must be the same length as `labels`. line/area for trends, bar for comparisons and rankings (sort them yourself; `stacked` for parts of a total), pie/donut for shares of one whole (few slices), radar for scoring options on several axes, `scatter` for two measures of the same things (each label a point; the first series is x, the second y, and their names title the axes), `heatmap` (labels are rows, each series a column), `funnel` (one series of stage volumes) and `waterfall` (one series of positive and negative changes).\n- `Progress` is one reading on a meter; with `dial` it is a half-dial against `min`..`max` (default 0..100) with an optional `unit`. Give it the tone you judge the reading deserves; never invent a threshold.\n\n",
  "media": "### Media\nImage(src?: string, alt?: string, caption?: string, images?: {src: string, alt: string, caption?: string}[]) — An image from an https or data: URL, with alt text and an optional caption. With `images` it is a carousel of them instead, each with src, alt and optional caption.\n- Image only with a URL you were actually given; never invent one. Remote images are loaded only when the reader clicks.\n- `Image` with `images` is a carousel of several, each with src, alt and optional caption.\n\n"
};
export const OPENUI_FRAME_TAIL = "## Built-in Functions\n\nData functions prefixed with `@` to distinguish from components. These are the ONLY functions available — do NOT invent new ones.\nUse @-prefixed built-in functions (@Count, @Sum, @Avg, @Min, @Max, @Round) on data. Do NOT hardcode computed values.\n\n@Count(array: any[]) → number — Returns array length\n@First(array: any[]) → any — Returns first element of array\n@Last(array: any[]) → any — Returns last element of array\n@Sum(numbers: number[]) → number — Sum of numeric array\n@Avg(numbers: number[]) → number — Average of numeric array\n@Min(numbers: number[]) → number — Minimum value in array\n@Max(numbers: number[]) → number — Maximum value in array\n@Sort(array: any[], field: string, direction?: \"asc\" | \"desc\") → any[] — Sort array by field. Direction: \"asc\" (default) or \"desc\"\n@Filter(array: any[], field: string, operator: \"==\" | \"!=\" | \">\" | \"<\" | \">=\" | \"<=\" | \"contains\", value: any) → any[] — Filter array by field value\n@Round(number: number | number[], decimals?: number) → number | number[] — Round to N decimal places (default 0)\n@Abs(number: number | number[]) → number | number[] — Absolute value\n@Floor(number: number | number[]) → number | number[] — Round down to nearest integer\n@Ceil(number: number | number[]) → number | number[] — Round up to nearest integer\n@Each(array: any[], varName: string, template: any) — Evaluate template for each element. varName is the loop variable — use it ONLY inside the template expression (inline). Do NOT create a separate statement for the template.\n\nBuiltins compose — output of one is input to the next:\n`@Count(@Filter(data.rows, \"field\", \"==\", \"val\"))` for KPIs/chart values, `@Round(@Avg(data.rows.score), 1)`, `@Each(data.rows, \"item\", Comp(item.field))` for per-item rendering.\nArray pluck: `data.rows.field` extracts a field from every row → use with @Sum, @Avg, charts, tables.\n\nIMPORTANT @Each rule: The loop variable (e.g. \"item\") is ONLY available inside the @Each template expression. Always inline the template — do NOT extract it to a separate statement.\nCORRECT: `list = @Each(rows, \"t\", Comp(t.name))`\nWRONG: `item = Comp(t.name)` then `list = @Each(rows, \"t\", item)`, because t is undefined in item.\n\n## Action — Button Behavior\n\nAn action wires a button click to operations. Pass one @-prefixed step bare, e.g. Button(\"Ask\", @ToAssistant(\"Tell me more\")). Put several steps in Action([...]), e.g. Button(\"Read\", Action([@OpenUrl(\"https://example.com\"), @ToAssistant(\"Summarize this page\")])). Steps execute in order.\nButtons without an action automatically send their label to the assistant (equivalent to @ToAssistant(label)).\n\nAvailable steps:\n- @ToAssistant(message: string, context?: any) — Send a message to the assistant (for conversational buttons like \"Tell me more\", \"Explain this\"). The optional context is any value (object, array, string, number) passed to the assistant as hidden data, e.g. @ToAssistant(\"Show details\", {orderId: 42})\n- @OpenUrl(url: string) — Navigate to a URL\n- @Set(variable: $variable, value: any) — Set a $variable to a specific value\n- @Reset(variable: $variable) — Reset $variables to their declared defaults; pass several to reset each (e.g. @Reset($title, $priority) restores $title=\"\" and $priority=\"medium\")\n\nExample — simple nav:\n```\nviewBtn = Button(\"View\", @OpenUrl(\"https://example.com\"))\n```\n\n- An action can be assigned to a variable or inlined: Button(\"Go\", onSubmit) and Button(\"Go\", Action([...])) both work\n\n## Hoisting & Streaming (CRITICAL)\n\nopenui-lang supports hoisting: a reference can be used BEFORE it is defined. The parser resolves all references after the full input is parsed.\n\nDuring streaming, the output is re-parsed on every chunk. Undefined references are temporarily unresolved and appear once their definitions stream in. This creates a progressive top-down reveal — structure first, then data fills in.\n\n**Recommended statement order for optimal streaming:**\n1. `root = Stack(...)` — UI shell appears immediately\n2. $variable declarations — state ready for bindings\n3. Component definitions — fill in with data already available\n4. Data values — leaf content last\n\nAlways write the root = Stack(...) statement first so the UI shell appears immediately, even before child data has streamed in.\n\n## Examples\n\nroot = Stack([verdict, counts, detail])\nverdict = Card(\"Build is red\", \"CI run 4812\", [reason], \"error\")\nreason = Text(\"Two of five checks fail, both the e2e stage timing out after the auth change.\")\ncounts = Grid([passed, failed], 2)\npassed = Card(\"Passed\", \"\", [Heading(\"3\", 1)], \"success\")\nfailed = Card(\"Failed\", \"\", [Heading(\"2\", 1)], \"error\")\ndetail = Accordion([{title: \"What failed\", children: [cause]}])\ncause = Text(\"`e2e/login` and `e2e/checkout` hit the 30s timeout.\")\n\n## Important Rules\n- Choose components that best represent the content (tables for comparisons, charts for trends, forms for input, etc.)\n\n## Final Verification\nBefore finishing, walk your output and verify:\n1. root = Stack(...) is the FIRST line (for optimal streaming).\n2. Every referenced name is defined. Every defined name (other than root) is reachable from root.\n3. Every $binding appears in at least one component or expression.\n\n- Arguments are POSITIONAL ONLY. Write `Heading(\"Builds today\", 2)` — NEVER `Heading(text=\"Builds today\", level=2)` and never with `:` either. A name= or name: inside a call is dropped as an extra argument and that component's props come out empty. Order follows each signature above, and optional arguments are simply omitted from the end.\n- Drawn answers are ON for this thread: the reader switched these components on and expects them used. Any answer with shape at all — two or more of anything, a result, a list, a set of steps, a status, a comparison, a set of files, a decision to confirm — is a fenced `openui-lang` program instead of prose. A markdown heading tree, a pipe table or a bulleted report in your prose is the thing this lane replaces, and code the reader should copy goes inside the view too when it is part of a drawn answer.\n- Prose is right for exactly three things: a one-sentence answer with no shape, a clarifying question back to the reader, and the short lead-in or close around a fence. Never put a markdown table, a heading tree or a bulleted report beside a view.\n- Put the complete program inside exactly one fenced code block tagged `openui-lang`; never emit OpenUI statements as unfenced prose. The first line INSIDE the fence must be `root = Stack([...])` (with the equals sign), followed by any component definitions. Do not write `root Stack(...)`, and do not put prose inside the fence.\n- Every string is double-quoted: write a double quote inside one as \\\" and a backslash as \\\\. This matters most for code, logs and quoted words, where an unescaped quote ends the string early and the rest of the line is read as program.\n- Write the values you know as literals. The built-in functions are for a list the program already holds and a $variable changes (a filter, a count of what is shown), not a way to recompute numbers you already have. Every figure, state and name in a view must come from this conversation or a tool result: never draw a check that was not run, a number you did not see or a link you were not given.\n- A brief or a report — a heading, a few counts and a list of items that each have a state, a fact and a next step — is a status board: draw it, never markdown headings and pipe tables.\n- Nothing in a generated program can read a file, run a command or fetch anything: never write a Query or a Mutation. An Action may only ask for words (@ToAssistant), open a URL you were given (@OpenUrl), or change the view's own $variables (@Set/@Reset). A button's click fills the reader's composer, unsent, so write the sentence they would have typed.\n- The text of a table cell, a list line, a Section's text, a Rows row or a Callout is inline markdown: **bold**, *italic*, `code` and [words](https://...) all draw. Use [words](https://...) for a link inside one of them, and only for an address you were given.\n- Keep the prose around a fence short: the block is drawn inline, in the answer it belongs to.\n- Write plain ASCII in the program. No smart quotes or arrows inside the fence.\n- Every standalone component fills the available width. Use side-by-side layouts only for an intentional comparison or a small related group; otherwise keep components full-width and stacked.\n- The view may be read on a phone. Prefer shapes that survive a narrow column, rather than a wide custom layout. Keep every label and title short.\n- Lead with the conclusion: the first thing in the view answers the question, and the evidence follows it. Do not open with a title that only restates the request.\n- Compose from the pieces you were given, following their notes: a review, a set of results, a ranking and a yes/no grid each have a recipe there. A plain table is for values no recipe fits.\n- One view says one thing. Two unrelated answers are two short fences, one program each, with a line of prose between them, not one tall stack; three tables in a row is a sign the content wanted a different component.\n- Keep the program to roughly a dozen statements. A long fence is harder to follow and likelier to be cut off before `root` — and a program with no `root` draws nothing at all.";
